From 02f72d208333932bd07b7931a85aae614d8177f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 02:57:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/10] feat: attention becomes a derived, fail-honest contract
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Attention is what depends on the client right now. It is derived on every
request from the domains that already authorise themselves — there is no
attention_items table, no queue, no dismiss state and no persistence.

The contract carries the decision, not an exception: state is one of
attention, calm or degraded, plus a complete flag and two counters. Nothing
technical crosses the RSC boundary, so a component can render the answer
without knowing why a read failed.

The distinction that matters is between zero items because everything was
checked and zero items because a read failed. The first is calm; the second
is degraded, and calling it calm would tell a client nothing is waiting for
them while a pending item sits unread. The ternary tests failure before
calm, and a test breaks if anyone shortens it.

Isolating a source has a trap: notFound() and redirect() signal by throwing,
so a naive catch turns a cross-tenant 404 into "we could not check your
pending items" while the page keeps mounting around it. That is a security
failure wearing the costume of resilience. unstable_rethrow is the first
line of the catch — verified to exist in the Next version this project
pins — and a dedicated test asserts both signals still propagate.

The kind union declares only what exists. Nothing is anticipated because it
appears in the roadmap: a kind is born when there is a real client-facing
state, a real action, and an action the client can actually take.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
